+# Summary: `evlog report --since`
+
+## What changed
+- `evlog/cli.py`: `report` accepts `--since DATE`. The value is parsed with `datetime.fromisoformat`; an unparseable value is a usage error (exit 2, one-line message, no traceback). A missing or unreadable events file now prints `evlog: cannot read <path>: ...` to stderr and exits 1 instead of a traceback.
+- `evlog/events.py`: added `as_utc()` and `since()`. Timestamps are normalised to UTC before comparing, because the file mixes offset-aware timestamps with naive ones (naive = UTC, per README). Without this, comparing naive and aware datetimes raises `TypeError`.
+- `tests/test_since.py` (new): 13 tests in total for the `--since` behaviour. They cover the UTC midnight boundary, inclusion of the given day, offset and naive timestamps on both sides of the comparison, zero matches, invalid and missing `--since` values, and a missing file.
+- `README.md`: documents `--since`, the UTC day boundary, and the error exit status.
+- I did not edit or delete `tests/test_report.py`. Its `test_since_excludes_older_days` was failing before the change (no `--since` option) and passes now.
+
+## Choices I made (please confirm)
+- The day boundary is 00:00 UTC, and the cutoff is inclusive. An event at 23:00 UTC on the 11th is excluded from `--since 2024-03-12` even if its local date is the 12th. The teammate's test expects this UTC reading as well.
+- `--since` also accepts full ISO timestamps (for example `2024-03-12T06:00:00+09:00`), since `fromisoformat` already parses them. This is a superset of "a date".
+
+## Verification
+- `python -m unittest -v` (Python 3.14.3): 13 tests, all OK. Baseline before the change: 2 tests, 1 error (the `--since` test).
+- `python -m evlog report /nonexistent/events.jsonl`: prints `evlog: cannot read ...`, exit 1.
+- `python -m evlog report /dev/null --since 2024-13-01`: argparse usage error, exit 2, no traceback.
+- End-to-end on a three-line sample file kept outside the repo, `--since 2024-03-10`: `api error 2`, `worker info 1`, `total 3`. I checked this by hand: the `-05:00` event is 04:30 UTC on the 10th, and the naive `01:00` event is UTC, so both count. Without `--since` the same file gives all three events.
+
+## Not verified / open points
+- Only Python 3.14 was tested. `datetime.fromisoformat` accepts more formats from 3.11 on, so older interpreters may reject some inputs that this accepts. I did not check the minimum supported version, because the project does not declare one.
+- Malformed JSON lines or missing `ts`/`service`/`level` fields in the events file still produce a traceback. I left this alone: it is file content, not command-line input, and it is outside the scope you described.
+- I did not run the CLI under a real ops export; only the sample file above.
+- Run `python -m unittest` once more on your side. I removed the `__pycache__` directories my runs created, and the repo has no `.gitignore`, so they may reappear.
